You are analyzing code for naming convention consistency and comment coverage. Complete the following steps.

Step 1: Write the following JavaScript code to a file named `app.js`:

```javascript
const UserName = 'Alice';
const user_age = 30;
let ITEM_count = 5;
var TotalPrice = 99.99;

// Calculate discount
function calcDisc(p, d) {
  return p - (p * d);
}

function GetUserInfo(id) {
  return { id: id, name: UserName };
}

function update_user_record(userId, newData) {
  // update the record
  return Object.assign({}, newData);
}

function ProcessOrderItems(items) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    total += items[i].price;
  }
  return total;
}

const x = (a, b) => a + b;
```

Step 2: Analyze the code and produce a naming and documentation report. Save it to `naming_report.json` with exactly this structure:

```json
{
  "total_lines": <integer>,
  "comment_lines": <integer>,
  "comment_rate": <float rounded to 2 decimal places, as a percentage 0-100>,
  "naming_style_violations": [
    {
      "identifier": "string",
      "current_style": "string",
      "expected_style": "camelCase" | "snake_case" | "UPPER_SNAKE_CASE" | "PascalCase",
      "suggestion": "string"
    }
  ],
  "dominant_naming_style": "camelCase" | "snake_case" | "PascalCase",
  "comment_rating": "excellent" | "good" | "fair" | "poor",
  "recommendations": ["string"]
}
```

Count total non-empty lines in the file (excluding blank lines). Count lines that start with `//`. Calculate comment_rate as (comment_lines / total_lines * 100) rounded to 2 decimal places. Identify at least 3 naming style violations (identifiers that deviate from camelCase convention for JavaScript variables/functions). Set comment_rating based on the comment_rate: <5% = poor, 5-15% = fair, 15-25% = good, >25% = excellent.
